The rewrite below should return the same values as the original. I haven't benchmarked it. The changes:

- **`GetType()` once:** it is called a single time, where the original called it once or twice. If it is virtual, this saves the extra dispatch.
- **Raw byte pointer:** bytes are read through a plain pointer instead of indexing `values` on every iteration.
- **`memcpy` fast path:** when the stored base-type size equals `sizeof(T)` on a little-endian target, the value is read with one `memcpy`. This produces the same bit pattern as the original shift-and-OR loop.
- **Fallback:** any other size, or a big-endian target, uses the original loop unchanged.

```cpp
template <typename T>
T FieldBase::GetValue(const FIT_UINT8 fieldArrayIndex) const
{
    const FIT_UINT8 typeNum = GetType() & FIT_BASE_TYPE_NUM_MASK;
    const FIT_UINT8 size = baseTypeSizes[typeNum];
    if (fieldArrayIndex >= (FIT_UINT8)values.size())
    {
        return *reinterpret_cast<const T*>(baseTypeInvalids[typeNum]);
    }

    const FIT_BYTE* p = values.data() + (static_cast<size_t>(fieldArrayIndex) * size);

#if defined(__BYTE_ORDER__) && (__BYTE_ORDER__ == __ORDER_LITTLE_ENDIAN__)
    if (size == sizeof(T))
    {
        T rv;
        std::memcpy(&rv, p, sizeof(T));
        return rv;
    }
#endif

    T rv = 0;

    for (int i = 0; i < size; i++)
    {
        rv |= static_cast<T>(p[i]) << (i * 8);
    }

    return rv;
}
```

